UNIT parse_wire
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@f68cca701417 cli/lang/rust.mjs parseWire — the rust driver's hand-written reader of one harness output line; the code is the truth
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG parse_wire(line: text) -> json
ENVELOPE line is well-formed text (no unpaired surrogate in the line itself). Every JSON text is in scope, every malformed
  line is in scope (a throw), and so is every line R8 refuses for depth. OUT: a line the package ACCEPTS whose result nests
  more than 100 levels (no verifier transport carries a value that deep). Two result values lie beyond the abstract json
  and are verified only by the js envelope extension: the exact integer of R6, and text holding an unpaired surrogate (R3).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read one line the rust verification harness printed — a single JSON text — into the value it carries. It is
  RFC 8259 reading with three additions: an integer numeral no double equals comes back as that exact integer (so a u64 or
  i64 the harness printed is never rounded, and a huge integer is never infinite); a non-integer numeral with no finite
  double throws; and a value nested more than 128 levels down throws.
BEHAVIOR
R1 line must be exactly one JSON text (RFC 8259 grammar) and nothing else: blanks — and only the four JSON blanks SPACE,
  TAB, LF and CR — may surround it and any token inside it. Anything else -> throws: empty or blank-only text, trailing
  content after the value, a byte order mark, NBSP or any other whitespace character, a trailing comma, a missing colon or
  comma, an unquoted key, single quotes, a numeral with a leading zero, a leading '+', a bare '.', a lone '-', a missing
  fraction or exponent digit, NaN or Infinity, an unknown or truncated literal, an unterminated string or container, a raw
  character U+0000..U+001F inside a string, an unknown escape, a backslash ending the line, a backslash-u escape without
  four hex digits.
R2 The JSON literals "true" and "false" -> those bools; the literal "null" -> none. A list -> list, elements in order.
R3 A string -> text. An escape is a backslash and one more character: one of  "  /  b  f  n  r  t  or a second backslash
  stands for U+0022, U+002F, U+0008, U+000C, U+000A, U+000D, U+0009 and U+005C respectively; a backslash, u and four hex
  digits XXXX (either case) is the UTF-16 code unit XXXX — so a high-surrogate escape followed by a low-surrogate escape is
  one supplementary character, while an unpaired surrogate escape (or a low before a high) leaves those lone code units in
  the text. Every other character (U+2028, U+2029, DEL, non-ASCII, supplementary) is itself.
R4 An object -> map. A repeated key keeps its LAST value, yet every earlier value is still read by R1-R8, so one that
  throws throws the line ('{"a":1e400,"a":1}' throws). Every key is an ordinary key, including __proto__, constructor and
  __f64bits; keys spelling integers are ordinary keys too. A key is read by R3 exactly as a string value is, and what one
  line's keys read as never depends on any line read before it.
R5 A numeral with a fraction or an exponent -> f64: its decimal value rounded to the nearest double (ties to even), sign
  kept — so "-0.0", "-0e5" and "-1e-400" are negative zero, and an underflowing numeral is a zero of its sign. When that
  rounding gives no finite double (magnitude at or above 2^1024 - 2^970, e.g. "1e400", "1.7976931348623159e308") -> throws.
R6 INTEGER SPELLING (an optional '-' then digits; no fraction, no exponent). When some double equals the numeral's exact
  value, the result is that double (f64) — every integer up to 2^53 in magnitude, and the exactly representable ones beyond
  (2^53, 2^60, 2^64, 10^20); "-0" is negative zero. Otherwise the result is the numeral's EXACT integer, of unbounded width:
  2^53 + 1, 2^64 - 1, and an integer past the whole double range alike (never an infinity, never a throw). A fraction or
  exponent spelling never takes this path ("9007199254740993.0" is the double 9007199254740992) CITED FIDELITY package
R7 R5 and R6 apply at every depth: a top-level numeral, a list element and a map value alike.
R8 DEPTH. The top-level value is at depth 0; each element of a list and each value of a map is one deeper than its
  container. A value at depth above 128 -> throws, whatever it is — so 129 nested lists around a numeral throw, 130 nested
  empty lists throw, while 129 nested empty lists would be read (that result is outside ENVELOPE) CITED FIDELITY package
INVARIANT the result never holds a non-finite number; every exact integer of R6 has magnitude above 2^53.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN the abstract SIG takes text; any other argument -> throws before anything is read, and is never converted to
  text: none, a number, a bool, a list (even an empty one, or one holding the single text "[1]") and a map (with or without a
  "length" entry) alike. Verified by the js envelope extension only.
NOTE rust is in scope: its harness hands the artifact's serde_json::Value back through this very function, so the integer
  digits the artifact returns arrive exact at any width; the vectors that carry an R6 integer or a lone surrogate are js and
  ts only in the shared oracle, and rust's R6 integers are held to the js and ts results by cross-verification.
ORACLE value -> oracles/parse_wire.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 0dce5921a49cbad728d2c82ec5554e14f506b73f68b93ea08dbb81add2939b11
SCOPE IN: R1-R8 over ENVELOPE, and OUT-OF-DOMAIN (js only). OUT: accepted results deeper than 100 levels; the harness
  envelope check, reshape and f64 bit-wire decoding that follow this function in the driver.
